markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Reading JSON: Finding What the UI Needs

#### When an app shows information on the screen, it usually **doesn’t use every piece of data** it receives.

#### Often, the app gets a *big* response (like JSON from an API) and then **picks only the fields it needs** to display.

---

## Scenario: The App Gets “Too Much” Data

#### Imagine you’re building a simple classroom weather app for teachers.

#### The app should display a small card with:
- #### a **title**
- #### a **subtitle**
- #### **one key stat**

#### But the API returns *way more* than that.

> #### Your job: scan the UI card and the raw JSON below and notice how much data from the API that the app actually used.

<div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; justify-content:center; margin-top:10px; align-items:stretch;">

  <!-- UI CARD -->
<div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:340px; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.08); box-sizing:border-box;">
    <div style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">UI Card (What the user sees)</div>
<div style="font-size:13pt; color:#555; margin-bottom:12px;">A simple weather snapshot</div>

<div style="background:#f7f7f7; border-radius:10px; padding:12px; margin-bottom:10px;">
<div style="font-weight:bold; font-size:13pt;">🌤 City Weather</div>
<div style="font-size:12.5pt; color:#444; margin-top:6px;">Indianapolis, IN</div>
<div style="font-size:12.5pt; color:#666; margin-top:2px;">Partly Cloudy</div>

<div style="margin-top:10px; background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:10px; padding:10px; font-size:12.5pt;">
        <strong>Key stat:</strong> 72°F
      </div>
    </div>

<div style="font-size:12pt; color:#444;">
      Notice: the card is small, but the data the app receives might be large.
</div>
  </div>

  <!-- JSON BOX -->
<div style="background:#ffffff; border:1px solid #ddd; border-radius:12px; padding:14px; width:340px; box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.08); box-sizing:border-box;">
<div style="font-size:14pt; font-weight:bold; margin-bottom:8px;">Raw JSON (What the app receives)</div>
 <div style="font-size:13pt; color:#555; margin-bottom:12px;">Unfiltered response from an API</div>

<div style="background:#111; color:#f1f1f1; border-radius:10px; padding:12px; font-size:10.5pt; line-height:1.35; overflow:auto; max-height:260px; white-space:pre;">
{
  "location": {
    "city": "Indianapolis",
    "state": "IN",
    "zip": "46204",
    "latitude": 39.7684,
    "longitude": -86.1581,
    "timezone": "America/Indiana/Indianapolis"
  },
  "current": {
    "temperature": 72,
    "condition": "Partly Cloudy",
    "feels_like": 74,
    "humidity": 56,
    "wind_speed": 8,
    "wind_direction": "NW",
    "uv_index": 3
  },
  "forecast": [
    { "day": "Monday", "high": 75, "low": 60, "condition": "Sunny" },
    { "day": "Tuesday", "high": 70, "low": 58, "condition": "Rain" }
  ],
  "data_source": "National Weather Service",
  "last_updated": "2026-02-25T10:32:00Z"
}
</div>

<div style="font-size:12pt; color:#444; margin-top:10px;">
      This data is structured — but not all of it will be used for this screen.
</div>
</div>

</div>

<div style="background-color:#B7E0E5; border-radius:10px; padding:14px; margin:18px auto; max-width:85%; box-sizing:border-box;">
  <p style="font-size:14pt; margin:0; line-height:130%;">💡 <strong>Key idea:</strong> Apps pick out only the pieces of JSON they need to display in the UI — the rest may be “nice to have” or “extra.”</p>
</div>

---

## Reflect: What would you pull from the JSON?

#### Take a moment to compare the UI card and the JSON.
#### 👉 If you were building this app, which **three pieces of information** would you want from this JSON?
#### 👉 What looks **useful** vs **extra** for *this specific* UI card?

<details style="background-color:#ececec; padding:10px; margin-bottom:10px; border:1px solid transparent; border-radius:8px;">
<summary style="font-size:1.4em; font-weight:bold;">💡 Check your thinking (click to reveal)</summary>

<div style="margin-top:12px; margin-left:14px; margin-right:14px; margin-bottom:10px; font-size:13pt; line-height:145%;">

<p style="margin-top:0; font-size:14pt;">
Your answers might include ideas like these:
</p>

<ul>
  <li style="font-size:14pt;">
    <strong>Match the UI card first.</strong><br>
    The card shows a location name, a condition, and one number. So you’d likely grab:
    <ul>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;"><code style="font-size:12pt;">location.city</code></li>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;"><code style="font-size:12pt;">location.state</code></li>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;"><code style="font-size:12pt;">current.condition</code></li>
      <li style="font-size:14pt;"><code style="font-size:12pt;">current.temperature</code></li>
    </ul>
  </li>

  <li style="margin-top:10px; font-size:14pt;">
    <strong>Useful vs extra depends on the screen.</strong><br>
    Things like <code style="font-size:12pt;">humidity</code> or <code style="font-size:12pt;">wind_speed</code> might be useful in a different version of the app, but they are not shown on this card.
  </li>

  <li style="margin-top:10px; font-size:14pt;">
    <strong>Extra fields aren’t “bad.”</strong><br>
    They’re just not needed <em>right now</em> for the UI being displayed.
  </li>
</ul>

<div style="background:#ffffff; border-radius:10px; padding:12px; margin-top:12px; border:1px solid #ddd;">
  <strong>Takeaway:</strong> JSON is organized data. Your app reads the structure and pulls only the fields that match what the user sees.
</div>

</div>
</details>


---

## Next

#### Next, we’ll practice reading the “shape” of JSON so we can grab exactly what our app needs.
MARKDOWN
